WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:04.650
We're gonna talk about the game of Amazons. It was invented in Argentina

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:11.420
by a chap named Walter Zamkauskas, I believe in 1980's. So it's a fairly new game.

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:15.980
For purposes of this video, I think we should use

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:21.600
only a 6x6 board because this makes the game faster. This is a good game for kids at that size.

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:26.060
The official size preferred by Zamkauskas is 10x10,

00:00:26.529 --> 00:00:33.179
which is also the size of a checkerboard in Argentina. And you can also play an 8x8 or, you could play any size

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:35.189
or could even be a rectangular board.

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:40.770
Well, there's two black pieces and two white pieces and there are various possible starting configurations.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.020
This is one that I like.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:47.220
A legal move is - let's just take one piece here.

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:51.800
A legal move is - by an amazon, if you choose to move this amazon,

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:58.180
it can go as far as it wants in any direction either diagonal or horizontal or vertical,

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:00.560
like a chess queen.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:05.780
Now after it moves it must shoot an arrow and the arrow also goes like a chess queen.

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:11.909
The same Amazon that moved must also shoot so maybe it shoots like that, okay.

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:15.689
Now the square in which it lands, this is like a flaming arrow,

00:01:15.689 --> 00:01:22.020
it is burned off the board and that square cannot be occupied nor can any other Amazon jump over it.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:23.480
Or...

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:27.240
capture it. There's no capturing, all the Amazons are immortal.

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:33.900
They run about shooting flaming arrows, and eventually the board gets blocked up with flaming arrows, and

00:01:34.479 --> 00:01:35.710
somebody can't move.

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:43.019
And when that happens, because they're unable to move then the game's over. And the player unable to move is the player who loses.

00:01:43.479 --> 00:01:46.349
(Brady: Or the player who did the last legal move is the winner?)

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:48.080
That's right.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:49.220
(Brady: Okay)

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:50.100
Yes

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:50.940
(Brady: Sounds fun)

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:55.640
Okay, well, it is. Brady's gonna play black and I'm gonna play white and we're gonna play a game.

00:01:55.980 --> 00:02:00.960
Well, let's say you can go first. It is believed there is an advantage to going first but no one really knows.

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:05.600
(Brady: I'm gonna go, so I'll go... there.)

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:09.500
(And then so I could shoot my arrow, for example, to there?)

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:11.500
Sure.

00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:18.939
That's okay. All right I'll go here. Aaand shoot there.

00:02:21.859 --> 00:02:23.859
(Brady: I'm gonna go to...)

00:02:25.159 --> 00:02:27.159
(here.)

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:29.530
(Shoot my arrow to there.)

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:33.010
Okay

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:38.880
So I'll go here and shoot there.

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:46.700
(Brady: I'm feeling that black player getting a bit penned in there. So for example, I might, I might move to here.)

00:02:46.730 --> 00:02:50.409
(And then try and start penning in your white, your white amazon there)

00:02:51.049 --> 00:02:55.209
Yep, that's highly plausible. So I'll go here and shoot...

00:02:56.389 --> 00:02:58.389
there, okay.

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:00.730
(Brady: I'll go here)

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:05.360
(And shoot to there. Was that a good or a bad move?)

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:09.940
Uhh...hard for me to tell.

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:14.349
I...think I better - Let's go here and shoot here.

00:03:16.639 --> 00:03:19.599
(Brady: Okay, my black player is getting quite pinned in there, isn't he?)

00:03:19.639 --> 00:03:20.139
Yeah

00:03:20.139 --> 00:03:22.509
(I feel like I need to get that guy out of trouble.)

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:26.480
(Because if - because I could get boxed in here quite easily couldn't I)

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:27.620
True.

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:29.800
(So I'm gonna--I'm gonna come down to here...)

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:31.800
(...and then...)

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:35.739
(...fire my arrow here.)

00:03:37.069 --> 00:03:39.279
Well that works to some extent

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:44.180
(Are you feeling good about your situation here?)

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:47.100
Yeah, I feel pretty good.

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:50.340
I'll go here...

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:56.440
So now the board is split into two games. There's one over here,

00:03:57.080 --> 00:04:00.400
in which I'm way ahead cause I have a lot of space and

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:02.520
you have only one.

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:06.419
And there's another game over here. This divides the board into two pieces

00:04:06.879 --> 00:04:11.548
(Brady: Is this common in, in, in games for games to get split into sub games like this?)

00:04:12.819 --> 00:04:18.100
Certainly in the kind of combinatorial games that we study, yes.

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:22.280
Because that's a big part of how we manage to do an analysis.

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:26.660
Because in the mathematics we can do an analysis of this side of the board,

00:04:26.669 --> 00:04:30.329
the result is not gonna be too complicated because this is fairly simple.

00:04:30.430 --> 00:04:35.370
This over here is still fairly complicated. But we can do an analysis over here.

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:37.030
We do another analysis over here.

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:41.850
And then we have ways of adding these two analyses together to get an analysis of the whole game.

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:44.420
(Brady: Is there a name for this, this, this technique?)

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:46.680
Divide and conquer

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:49.030
(It feels like I'm I'm pretty dead over this side now right?)

00:04:49.030 --> 00:04:51.180
Over here there's a lot more at stake.

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:55.340
Yeah, there's some squares back here you will probably get.

00:04:56.140 --> 00:04:59.420
And there's some squares down here that more likely I will get.

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:02.220
There's some of the middle that we'll be fighting over.

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:05.410
So, okay presumably these two will be fighting for a while.

00:05:05.410 --> 00:05:06.580
(Brady: It's my move at the minute though isn't it?)

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:07.400
Yes

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:10.320
(I'm gonna go here)

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:12.960
(and fire here.)

00:05:12.970 --> 00:05:16.679
Okay, so I think I will just go here and shoot here.

00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:22.320
So now I have one, two, three, four, five. You have one, two, three, four, five, six, seven.

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:27.900
Okay, unless I can move again which case I'm gonna come up here and argue with you.

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:31.700
But at the moment you have two moves more than I do here, probably,

00:05:32.830 --> 00:05:34.510
roughly that.

00:05:34.510 --> 00:05:37.780
And over here I have one, two, three, four, five, six.

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:42.060
Six or seven moves ahead of you.

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:45.900
(Brady: I'll go...here)

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:49.980
(and fire here.)

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:58.900
So, now, this is sort of interesting. If I come in after you, I,

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:00.920
you may cut me off.

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:10.619
So, I'm not sure what the best plan is here, I probably will do that anyway I'll come in and...

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:21.719
...yeah...I'm gonna be okay here too.

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:23.260
(Brady: The best I can do is - )
- You can, you can...

00:06:24.820 --> 00:06:27.720
Well then I'll just kill you. And still get three points extra.

00:06:30.879 --> 00:06:33.629
But if you don't I'll back up and get all these

00:06:34.449 --> 00:06:35.589
(Yeah.)

00:06:35.589 --> 00:06:39.020
(I'll go, I'll go here and fire...)

00:06:39.140 --> 00:06:40.060
(here.)

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:43.060
So then I come here and shoot here.

00:06:44.020 --> 00:06:51.839
(Okay, so I only have one possible move which is to go here and fire here.)

00:06:51.939 --> 00:06:53.199
All right

00:06:53.199 --> 00:06:56.549
And now I start using up my moves. So go here.

00:06:57.430 --> 00:07:01.889
(And now I can't move -- I can't move that, I can't move that. And I can't move that.)

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:06.840
And I still have one, two, three, four, five, six, seven, eight, nine.

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:10.320
(Brady: Even if you had no more moves, you've won the game there)

00:07:10.460 --> 00:07:13.420
That's right. That's right. But in some sense we say I've won by nine points.

00:07:13.839 --> 00:07:19.618
There's only, there's only one game against a beginner I've ever lost out of somewhere like 40 now.

00:07:20.559 --> 00:07:24.419
(Brady: You should have told me that before I would have, I would have tried harder if I knew the stakes were so high)

00:07:25.930 --> 00:07:32.219
Jujo Jiang, who is a famous 9 dan go player, beat me the first Game of Amazons we played.

00:07:32.219 --> 00:07:34.949
Although I challenged him to rematch and I won the second.

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:39.980
There are a number of levels at which, uh, it helps teach mathematics.

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:45.560
For very small children, the beginners, just observing that we have, we have addition here.

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:50.600
We have one two, three, four, five six seven moves for white here

00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:52.880
and two moves here. So he wins by nine points.

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:56.960
Let's, let's, let's assume this end had some different way. Something like that, for instance.

00:07:57.650 --> 00:07:59.800
Suppose, suppose the game it ended like this. See?

00:08:00.950 --> 00:08:02.950
(Brady: Ooh, I would have preferred that.)

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:07.480
Well black has 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7 moves,

00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:11.480
white has 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7 moves.

00:08:11.480 --> 00:08:14.560
But white has another one so white is ahead by one point.

00:08:14.780 --> 00:08:18.610
White is one move ahead. So he will win even if he has to go first.

00:08:19.550 --> 00:08:22.623
Now if after this for instance--

00:08:22.623 --> 00:08:27.380
it's now 7 vs. 7 and the mover will lose because this adds up to zero.

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:32.120
So in general we say a game is worth zero when the mover cannot win.

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:36.184
So a zero game is one which is a win for the second player.

00:08:36.184 --> 00:08:39.800
In the original game, as prescribed by a Zamkauskas,

00:08:40.660 --> 00:08:45.203
is on a 10x10 board and each side starts with 4 Amazons.

00:08:45.203 --> 00:08:48.080
On an 8x8, we usually have 3 against 3,

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:51.760
and on a 6x6 it's usually two against two.

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:58.100
When, when we have an experienced player playing against the beginner, as I just did against Brady,

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:02.320
it'd be a more interesting game if I gave him three Amazon's against my two.

00:09:02.980 --> 00:09:05.700
(Brady: So that's - sometimes people play that way, they handicap them?)

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:08.460
Sure, sure. That's one way to handicap.

00:09:08.469 --> 00:09:14.100
The other is just: find a starting position in which one side's already more constrained than the other.

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:18.480
And so you can start from positions that are biased in one direction or the other.

00:09:18.530 --> 00:09:22.899
Well, yeah, I think it's an interesting game. I, I think the opening's are -

00:09:23.540 --> 00:09:25.620
I don't understand 'em. I don't think anybody does.

00:09:26.180 --> 00:09:29.640
It's a game that is played. There have been international tournaments.

00:09:29.643 --> 00:09:35.290
Back in the early days of Amazons they were played by fax and now I'm sure everything's computerised.

00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:38.949
There are people do research in it, yes. There are,

00:09:39.460 --> 00:09:46.260
Martin Muller at the University of Alberta has done a fair amount of work, as have some other people in,

00:09:46.260 --> 00:09:49.860
in trying to evaluate sizes of various positions and

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:54.600
I think by now it's sort of known how to play on very small boards, maybe 4x4.

00:09:55.220 --> 00:09:59.560
I don't think 6x6 is really understood yet.

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:04.960
The board has no direction. I mean all, all directions are equally good.

00:10:05.560 --> 00:10:09.040
In that sense it's more like go than like chess or checkers where

00:10:09.100 --> 00:10:16.260
there is a forward component built into the game. This game has no forward component.

00:10:16.700 --> 00:10:23.060
Zamkauskas' starting position has the four blacks on one side and lined up against the four on the other.

00:10:23.850 --> 00:10:30.799
Which I think is a little distracting for beginners because you - it sort of conveys this notion of

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:33.297
forward motion, coming at each other

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:40.300
but after a few moves each side gets around behind the other usually and it's, it's, it's up for grabs.

00:10:40.300 --> 00:10:42.460
There is no - the board really has no direction.

00:10:45.500 --> 00:10:48.220
[Preview] The region here is zero/zero because there's no Amazons,

00:10:48.600 --> 00:10:51.360
but it turns out each of these positions

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:56.549
is worth a half a point for a black. Run a chain through here

00:10:57.550 --> 00:11:00.240
and the only way you're gonna be able to stop it is to sacrifice a box.

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:05.250
Don't don't give away any sacrifice until you have to. Stop the side chains right away.

